WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:04.720
Because of climate change, mountain glaciers are melting rapidly,

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.700
and affecting numerous ecosystems,

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:13.050
but getting up-close readings
on these high-altitude environments is difficult.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:19.020
Satellite images don’t have enough detail,
and can be easily thwarted by clouds.

00:00:19.029 --> 00:00:25.050
Airplanes can map glaciers in high resolution,
but the cost can be astronomical.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:29.400
Oliver Wigmore, a scientist at the University of Colorado,

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.820
has developed a new high-flying drone,

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:37.740
that can take images at altitudes of up
to 6000 meters above sea level.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:43.160
Normally, drones have trouble reaching these
heights, because the air is too thin

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:46.300
for their propellers to build up enough lift.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:50.200
But with this new design, weight is kept to a minimum.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:53.460
The tiny aircraft only weighs 2 kilograms,

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:58.060
and with 6 individual rotors each over a foot long,

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:01.520
generating lift shouldn’t be a problem.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:04.120
To give their UAV a shakedown cruise,

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:09.120
researchers took it to the heights of the Andes mountains in South America,

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:14.220
where 70% of the Earth’s tropical ice is located,

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.340
and melting glaciers are often what supplies freshwater

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.880
to communities in the summer.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.720
With the drone’s help, researchers are able
to get closeup pictures

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:27.840
and create detailed models of the glacier surface.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.860
Using drone surveys collected one year apart,
scientists are able see where,

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:35.400
and how quickly the glacier ice is melting.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:39.560
While the flights through the Andes were the
first test of the UAV,

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:42.620
it was only the first stop on a world tour.

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:47.510
The researchers are taking it to even greater
heights in the Andes, and are using this kind of

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:50.940
closeup data gathering for other environmental studies,

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.500
in places like the mountains of Colorado.

